{
	"nodes":[
		{
			"id":"acce87ec11de85a7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Heroic Excellence**: Acting with supreme virtue, above and beyond one’s duty. Their level of excellence transcends human nature; they are essentially like a god among men.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":-600,
			"width":420,
			"height":180,
			"color":"1"
		},
		{
			"id":"c11d38d6a54c386e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Excellence**: Acting perfectly virtuously, with the desires and reason in harmony. Doing the right thing feels natural and pleasant to them.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":-380,
			"width":420,
			"height":160,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"453b61ebf7775599",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Continence**: Reason fights desire and wins; although they may have bad desires, reason is able to overcome such desires. They do the right things, but it is a struggle.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":-180,
			"width":420,
			"height":160,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"cb99449d6bad56f5",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Incontinence**: Reason loses to desire, so although the person wants to do the right thing, they want to do something bad even more. They feel regret for acting badly.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":20,
			"width":420,
			"height":160,
			"color":"3"
		},
		{
			"id":"55c74c8c5954cc14",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Vice**: Reason is corrupted and aligns with desire, such that the person does wrong because they think that it is actually good. They do things to the excess or the deficient.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":220,
			"width":420,
			"height":180,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"f7233ebff087594e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Brutishness**: They lack reason entirely, such that they act solely on instinct or desire. They are outside the realm of human vice.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-240,
			"y":440,
			"width":420,
			"height":120,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"55af651b5aa7ab9a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Incontinence is better than vice since the licentious man (the man with vice) yields to pleasure out of choice, while the incontinent man yields to pleasure out of weakness of will.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":380,
			"y":-20,
			"width":380,
			"height":170
		},
		{
			"id":"2dfa442aaef9959f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Licentiousness is also incurable, while incontinence is curable.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":440,
			"y":220,
			"width":260,
			"height":120
		},
		{
			"id":"3e91bb25dcf6afc6",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"In the incontinent man, the “first principles of morality” is alive, though suppressed by desire. Such is not the case in the licentious man.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":880,
			"y":220,
			"width":340,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"43845a0a39c88c03",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"The continent man and the temperate man both desire, but while the former has to suppress their wrong desires with reason to do the right things, the latter naturally desires the right things.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-840,
			"y":-200,
			"width":400,
			"height":180
		},
		{
			"id":"2e65a80cd90c853a",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Both the continent man and the licentious man desires bodily pleasures, but while the former thinks that such a desire is wrong, the latter thinks that such a desire is right.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-820,
			"y":200,
			"width":380,
			"height":180
		}
	],
